<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · c. 1860 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Paloma</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song in the habanera rhythm written about 1860 by the Spanish composer Sebastián Iradier; it is one of the most-recorded songs ever.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Sebastián Iradier</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Sebastián Iradier</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Spanish and Cuban (an early written habanera)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>written after Iradier's visit to Cuba; registered in Madrid, Spain</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1860</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (written by Sebastián Iradier around 1860, who died a few years later; registered in Madrid in 1879)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;La Paloma&quot; (&quot;The Dove&quot;) was written by the Spanish Basque composer Sebastián Iradier (earlier spelt Yradier) around 1860, after a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. It is one of the first written examples of the Afro-Cuban habanera rhythm to become widely popular. In 1879 it was registered at the copyright office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> as a &quot;Canción Americana con acompañamiento de Piano&quot; (an American song with piano accompaniment). Iradier died in obscurity within a few years of writing it and never learned how popular it would become.</p><p>The song soon spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, above all to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and from there around the world. German and French versions appeared in the 1860s, and in the Portuguese novel O Crime do Padre Amaro, written in 1871 by José Maria de Eça de Queirós, it is called &quot;la Chiquita, an old Mexican song&quot;. In places as far apart as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, Colombia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, Venezuela, Zanzibar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> it came to be treated almost as a folk song. There are more than a thousand versions; with the Beatles' &quot;Yesterday&quot; it is counted among the most-recorded songs in the history of music, and it is the most-recorded Spanish song.</p><p>Its image of the dove is traced back to 492 BCE, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>'s invasion of Greece, when the Persian fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> was wrecked in a storm off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> saw white doves, probably homing pigeons carried by the fleet, escaping from the sinking ships. From this came the idea that such birds carry home a last message of love from a sailor lost at sea.</p><p>In the 20th century the song was taken up by opera singers, jazz and pop musicians, rock and folk performers and military bands. Harry James recorded it in 1941; &quot;No More&quot;, with English words by Don Robertson and Hal Blair, was written for Elvis Presley to sing in the film Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> (1961) and was also recorded by Dean Martin; Connie Francis's French version &quot;Jamais&quot; (1961) reached the top ten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; Brendan Bowyer's version reached No. 1 in Ireland in 1963; and Mireille Mathieu's &quot;La Paloma, Adieu&quot; (1973) was a hit across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In films it was sung by Hans Albers in a German version in Große Freiheit Nr. 7, which Nazi censorship kept from German screens in 1944 and which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> released in 1945, and by Rosita Serrano in Das Boot (1981); it plays in The Godfather Part II (1974) at the New Year party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. On 9 May 2004 a choir of 88,600 people sang it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, a Guinness world record, and Sigrid Faltin's documentary La Paloma. Sehnsucht. Weltweit (2008) is about the song.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light-hearted love song in the habanera rhythm. A man leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> asks his sweetheart to treat kindly a dove that comes to her window, for the dove is himself, and later verses look forward playfully to their wedding and a houseful of children. Behind the image lies the old belief that a dove carries home a last message of love from a sailor lost at sea.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Cuando salí de La Habana,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When I left Havana,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Válgame Dios!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God help me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Nadie me ha visto salir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nobody saw me leave</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">si no fui yo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but myself,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">y una linda Guachinanga</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and a pretty Guachinanga</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Sí, allá voy yo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, there I go,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que se vino tras de mí,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who came along after me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Que sí, señor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh yes, sir!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Si a tu ventana llega</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If to your window comes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">una Paloma,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a dove,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">trátala con cariño</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>treat it with affection,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que es mi persona.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for it is my own self.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Cuéntale tus amores,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tell it of your loves,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">bien de mi vida,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dear one of my life,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">corónala de flores</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>crown it with flowers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que es cosa mía.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for it is a thing of mine.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Ay! ¡Chinita que sí!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! Chinita, oh yes!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Ay! ¡Que dame tu amor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! Oh give me your love!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Ay! Que vente conmigo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! Oh come with me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">chinita, a donde vivo yo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>chinita, to where I live!</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 44 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El día que nos casemos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The day that we marry</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Válgame Dios!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God help me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En la semana que hay ir,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the week when we must go [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">me hace reír,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it makes me laugh,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">desde la Iglesia juntitos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the church close together,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que sí señor,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>oh yes, sir,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">nos iremos a dormir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we will go off to sleep.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Allá voy yo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There I go.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Cuando el curita nos eche</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the little priest gives us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">la bendición</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the blessing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">en la iglesia Catedral</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the Cathedral church</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">allá voy yo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there I go,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">yo te daré la manita</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will give you my little hand</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">con mucho amor</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with much love</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">y el cura dos hisopazos.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the priest two sprinkles of holy water.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Que sí, señor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh yes, sir!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Cuando haya pasado tiempo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When time has passed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Válgame Dios!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God help me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De que estemos casaditos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>since we were wed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">pues sí señor,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why yes, sir,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">lo menos tendremos siete,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the least we will have seven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Y que furor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And what a frenzy!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">O quince guachinanguitos…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Or fifteen little guachinangos…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Allá voy yo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There I go!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Sebastián Iradier (c. 1860); as quoted in Spanish Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_paloma_(canci%C3%B3n)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>Válgame Dios, literally 'may God avail me', an exclamation: 'God help me!'.</li><li>Allá voy yo, literally 'there I go': 'I am coming', 'here I come'.</li><li>Lines 1.5 and 5.7: Guachinanga, guachinanguitos: in Cuban Spanish guachinango was a name for a Mexican, and the word is usually read so here [?].</li><li>Line 2.4: es mi persona, literally 'it is my person', i.e. it is I.</li><li>Lines 2.9 and 2.12: chinita, a term of endearment for a girl or woman, not a name.</li><li>Line 3.3: 'en la semana que hay ir' is unclear: perhaps 'in the week we have to go'.</li><li>Line 4.1: curita, 'little priest', an affectionate diminutive of cura.</li><li>Line 4.7: hisopazos, strokes of the hisopo, the aspergillum that sprinkles holy water.</li><li>Line 5.6: furor, 'fury' or 'frenzy'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="clE-hvcmsyY" data-title="La Paloma: Victoria de los Ángeles, soprano, with orchestra (arr. Gamley)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Victoria de los Ángeles, soprano, with orchestra (arr. Gamley)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Victoria de los Ángeles - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=clE-hvcmsyY" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">No More (Don Robertson and Hal Blair)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new English words, sung by Elvis Presley in Blue Hawaii</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jamais (Connie Francis)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">French version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Paloma, Adieu (Mireille Mathieu)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1973</span><span class="sg-chain-h">French version, released elsewhere as &quot;La Paloma, Adé&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sing To Your Senorita (Down Argentine Way)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody loosely based on it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Paloma. Sehnsucht. Weltweit (Sigrid Faltin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">documentary about the song</span></li></ul></section>
